`io.nats:jnats`, MQTT via `eclipse-mosquitto`) is to spin up a Docker container by hand in `@BeforeAll` via `ProcessBuilder`, pinned to the exact tag in `tests/tck-build-logic/.../allowed-docker-images/`, rather than using Testcontainers — that library only appears in this repo when the library *under test* literally is Testcontainers. `pulsar-client-all`'s POM dependency list confirms it's a true uber-artifact (bouncycastle, opentelemetry, jackson-annotations, caffeine all transitively bundled), so the gap isn't a missing Maven coordinate — it's that produce/consume/schema code paths are simply unreachable without a live broker on `pulsar://localhost:6650`.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nResearch summary: checked the Maven Central POM for `pulsar-client-all:4.2.5` (no missing dependencies — it's self-contained), confirmed via Pulsar docs that the client needs a running broker at `pulsar://localhost:6650` for any meaningful produce/consume/schema/compression coverage, verified `apachepulsar/pulsar:4.2.5` exists on Docker Hub (exact version match, multi-arch), and cross-checked this repo's existing broker-client tests (`JnatsTest.java`, mosquitto) which all use a hand-rolled `docker run` in `@BeforeAll` rather than Testcontainers — no pulsar image is currently pinned in the allow-list.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"advisory_preparation\",\n  \"summary\": \"pulsar-client-all is the full client implementation (not just API interfaces); its producer/consumer, schema, and compression code paths only execute against a live Pulsar broker, so the generated test needs a standalone Pulsar Docker container, following this repo's existing pattern for jnats/mosquitto broker clients.\",\n  \"deterministic_setup\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"docker_image\",\n      \"image\": \"apachepulsar/pulsar:4.2.5\",\n      \"slug\": \"apachepulsar_pulsar\",\n      \"reason\": \"pulsar-client-all's connection, producer/consumer, schema, and compression (lz4/zstd/snappy) code is only reachable against a running broker; no in-process or mock broker exists. Tag matches the tested client version exactly and is published for amd64/arm64 on Docker Hub.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"agent_guidance\": \"In @BeforeAll, start the broker the same way JnatsTest.java and the mosquitto-based tests do in this repo: `new ProcessBuilder(\\\"docker\\\", \\\"run\\\", \\\"--rm\\\", \\\"-p\\\", \\\"6650:6650\\\", \\\"-p\\\", \\\"8080:8080\\\", \\\"apachepulsar/pulsar:4.2.5\\\", \\\"bin/pulsar\\\", \\\"standalone\\\").inheritIO().start()`, then poll until a PulsarClient can connect before running tests, and destroy the process in @AfterAll. Connect with `PulsarClient.builder().serviceUrl(\\\"pulsar://localhost:6650\\\").build()`. Give the readiness wait a materially longer timeout than the ~120s used for NATS/MQTT — standalone Pulsar bootstraps an embedded ZooKeeper and BookKeeper in addition to the broker, so it starts noticeably slower. No extra Maven dependency or system property is needed; pulsar-client-all already pulls in bouncycastle, opentelemetry, and jackson-annotations transitively.\",\n  \"risks\": [\n    \"Standalone Pulsar is heavier than the single-binary brokers already allow-listed (NATS, MQTT): it embeds ZooKeeper and BookKeeper, so container startup is slower and more memory-intensive, which could strain CI timeouts/resources relative to existing broker-client tests.\",\n    \"This introduces a brand-new Docker image entry (no existing pinned pulsar tag to reuse), so it needs its own allow-list addition rather than reusing a shared tag.\"\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":4048,"type":"result","duration_ms":135140,"uuid":"a23cc5f7-21f0-4723-b8de-10853ee199f2","ttft_stream_ms":1624,"time_to_request_ms":175,"first_content_frame_ms":1625,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

